Thank you.

49
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:33,880
So I think just to
recap and this--

50
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:36,520
the class is named as
self-improving agents.

51
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:38,460
So it's not just
focusing on LLMs.

52
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,740
It's focusing on the
agent aspect of it.

53
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:44,000
What that roughly-- if
we were to remind you

54
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,400
of what we discussed
in first class,

55
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:50,780
is that how is an agent
a generalization of LLM.

56
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:53,580
It has a goal and it will go and
interact with the environment,

57
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:56,980
collect feedback, and use that
feedback to correct its steps.

58
00:02:56,980 --> 00:02:58,560
So they're basically
systems that

59
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,600
can direct their own processes,
use tools, and then accomplish

60
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:03,640
a goal.

61
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:08,520
And oftentimes in the current
paradigm, the LLMs by themselves

62
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:11,600
are not powerful enough to
drive towards a full goal.

63
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:12,680
They're getting there.

64
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:16,760
So oftentimes we would
hand-write these systems

65
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:20,320
as workflows where you are
orchestrating LLMs and tools.

66
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:24,580
However, in certain
scenarios like coding agents,

67
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:27,120
you are seeing some of these
agentic workflows being

68
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,660
driven by themselves.

69
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:31,120
So it's an exciting
time where we are seeing

70
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:34,080
a lot of progress in this area.

71
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:37,440
And how we construct
these workflows oftentimes

72
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,680
means you're
orchestrating LLMs, you're

73
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,580
orchestrating verifiers because
you need some form of reward.

74
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:45,140
You might have LLMs as
judges for verifiers.

75
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:46,580
You might have tool calls.

76
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:48,820
You might have search
algorithms in there.

77
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:52,680
So you might have parallel LLM
calls that are going and doing

78
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:53,580
these kind of search.

79
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:56,040
But overall, since
an agentic workflow

80
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,400
is driving towards the goal, It.

81
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:02,620
Needs the capability to plan and
to reason over multiple steps,

82
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:05,680
correct itself if it were
going in the wrong direction,

83
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:10,000
and overall keep improving
in its capabilities, which

84
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,200
is where the self-improvement
aspect comes in.

85
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,500
So in this lecture,
as we wanted--

86
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:18,579
we wanted to cover some
future research areas.

87
00:04:18,579 --> 00:04:22,920
So we organized it as key
ideas from certain papers

88
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:25,960
that both cover the
self-improvement side of things

89
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:29,400
and just generally the
efficiency side of things

90
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:31,960
for intelligence, both
of which we believe

91
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:36,000
are extremely important
directions in the next research

92
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,960
areas.

93
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:44,480
So for the first three
papers that I will cover,

94
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:46,060
I'll just present the key ideas.

95
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:50,460
But let me just present why
those papers were chosen

96
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:55,400
and what are the key ideas
that they point in terms

97
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:57,020
of next steps and directions.

98
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:00,260
So what we learned in the
class is a lot about say,

99
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:04,160
test-time scaling,
where you could improve

100
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:08,560
by having multiple samples
and majority voting and so on,

101
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:10,000
and then train-time
scaling where

102
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,880
you can use that feedback
in the inference loop

103
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:17,320
and get rewards to drive
that self-improvement loop.

104
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:21,000
Oftentimes, this is still
limited to narrow domains

105
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,220
like math and coding.

106
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:25,640
So how do you get generalization
across these domains,

107
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:28,960
and how do you generally
get those reasoning chains

108
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:31,440
to be diverse enough so
that you can continue

109
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:34,240
to drive that self-improvement
loop is something

110
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:38,560
that is an important
research area even now.

111
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:43,280
An important aspect of building
these self-improvement loops

112
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:44,860
continues to be verification.

113
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:49,025
So how do you have robust
verification techniques or meta

114
00:05:49,025 --> 00:05:50,400
verification
techniques where you

115
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:53,400
verify what was going
into the reasoning

116
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:56,420
chain is often quite valuable.

117
00:05:56,420 --> 00:06:00,280
So that's another area that
the second paper I will present

118
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:01,760
the ideas on will cover.

119
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:04,640
And the third aspect
is that even though we

120
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:07,840
talk about train-time
scaling, the prompts that

121
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:12,400
go into training these models
gets selected very statically

122
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:14,800
and require humans
to select them.

123
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:17,720
So how do you break
through the data barriers

124
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:19,720
so that the
self-improvement loop

125
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:24,377
can pick the right set of
data that drives that loop?

126
00:06:24,377 --> 00:06:26,460
So that's going to be the
first part of the focus.

127
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:28,002
And the second part
of the focus will

128
00:06:28,002 --> 00:06:33,080
be focused on just what drives
efficiency for intelligence, so

129
00:06:33,080 --> 00:06:35,800
intelligence per what aspects.

130
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:37,760
So let's take a
quick look at some

131
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:41,160
of the ideas that might drive
this self-improvement loop

132
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:44,100
further in the next
generation of systems.

133
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:47,080
And given how fast the
field is moving, maybe

134
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:51,600
we'll be teaching papers
on each of this next time.

135
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:54,200
So the first paper
that we cover is

136
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:55,860
coming from
multi-agent finetuning,

137
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:57,560
which focuses on
self-improvement

138
00:06:57,560 --> 00:07:00,480
with diverse reasoning chains.

139
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:05,140
The key idea of this paper is
that pre-training, compute,

140
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:07,740
and instruction tuning
is bottle-necked by--

141
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:10,280
so pre-training compute is a
compression of internet scale

142
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:13,660
data and instruction finetuning
often requires real human data,

143
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:18,360
where you need human preferences
to decide what is good model

144
00:07:18,360 --> 00:07:21,520
response and bad
human response--

145
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:22,780
bad model response.

146
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:24,760
So an alternative
to this is that you

147
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:27,160
can use the data generated
by LLMs, which is often

148
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:28,262
called as synthetic data.

149
00:07:28,262 --> 00:07:29,720
And you can do
iterative finetuning

150
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:32,320
where you generate
possible solutions

151
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:33,840
and filter out the
incorrect ones.

152
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:35,840
This is called rejection
sampling and fine-tuned

153
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:37,140
only on the good ones.

154
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:39,200
Now there are many
variants, STaR

155
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:43,500
and so on, which can help
drive this loop even better.

156
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:45,440
We covered STaR in class.

157
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:49,360
You can even add reasoning
chains in that process.

158
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:51,680
But what typically
happens is that if you're

159
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:55,360
using a single large language
model to generate the data,

160
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:59,980
then it will generate solutions
that will be very similar.

161
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:02,580
And oftentimes the
performance increase

162
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:07,640
will stop after a few iterations
or after tens of iterations.

163
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:11,120
And typically, this
problem comes down

164
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:13,380
to really the notion
of lack of diversity.

165
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:16,180
So when you look
at model training,

166
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,680
at the pre-training
scale, the data

167
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:21,360
is so diverse because it was
generated over such a long time

168
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:22,100
by humans.

169
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:27,620
So it is quite diverse and
that helps the performance.

170
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:31,000
But when a single large language
model is generating outputs

171
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,320
for a set of prompts, it will
not have very diverse responses

172
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:36,600
even at high temperatures.

173
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:38,600
So the intuitive
solution for this

174
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:41,840
often means that you can
use multiple agents, which

175
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:44,400
are specialized in
some way, and then use

176
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:47,380
them to improve the diversity.

177
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:50,000
So what this paper
proposes, for example,

178
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,040
is multiple specialized
agents for generations

179
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:56,380
that will then produce a
diverse initial solutions.

180
00:08:56,380 --> 00:08:59,960
So they train generation
agents and critic agents.

181
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:03,120
So the generation agents
will help generate

182
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:05,060
a lot of diverse
initial solutions,

183
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:07,840
while the critic agents
will evaluate and refine

184
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:09,280
the solutions.

185
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:11,320
So those are
specialists as well.

186
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:14,500
So if you were to look
at step-by-step process,

187
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:17,360
and we don't need to go
through every single detail

188
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:20,960
in the algorithm below,
but the generation agent

189
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:23,600
will come up with
an initial answer

190
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:26,340
and then there will be
debate over multiple rounds.

191
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:29,080
So what the generation
agent will--

192
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:32,260
in the initial step, it will
just have the initial answer,

193
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:36,240
but in the subsequent steps,
it will summarize and get

194
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:38,600
a summary of all
other agents responses

195
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:43,560
and use that also as part of
generating the next response.

196
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:46,160
The goal of the critic
agent is to critique

197
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:47,860
this updated set of answers.

198
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:50,680
So basically, if you're
doing any iterations,

199
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:55,040
there's an initial response from
each of the generation agents.

200
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:57,700
Then you do a summary
across all of these agents,

201
00:09:57,700 --> 00:09:59,480
and then the critic
agent will critique

202
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:01,400
this updated set of answers.

203
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:05,460
So it's very similar to what you
guys were already familiar with.

204
00:10:05,460 --> 00:10:07,680
The main difference
here is that instead

205
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:13,060
of getting the critique over
a single agent's response,

206
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:15,480
you're actually using
multiple agents responses

207
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:19,860
and then specializing and
summarizing across them.

208
00:10:19,860 --> 00:10:24,380
What this enables is that you
have some form of diversity,

209
00:10:24,380 --> 00:10:26,200
even at the generation
stage, before you're

210
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:27,260
critiquing the answer.

211
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:29,880
So you basically get
majority voting for free

212
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:34,760
just by having multiple agents
and assuming that these are

213
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:36,500
trained slightly differently.

214
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:39,800
You get diversity for free.

215
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:42,840
So the generation models
in this particular case

216
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:45,340
are fine-tuned from
the same base model.

217
00:10:45,340 --> 00:10:49,220
And the goal there is to produce
good answers given a question.

218
00:10:49,220 --> 00:10:52,040
So over multiple iterations,
what they're doing is

219
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:54,240
they're taking
outputs and filtering

220
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:56,060
for a match with
the majority vote.

221
00:10:56,060 --> 00:11:00,720
And then doing SFT on this list
of prompt and response pairs.

222
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:03,720
If you were looking at a very
naive version of doing this,

223
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:05,300
you could just use
different models,

224
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:08,620
which is something that
people do in practice as well.

225
00:11:08,620 --> 00:11:10,880
So they will get very
different responses

226
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:11,900
with different prompts.

227
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:17,400
So that's the poor man's version
of doing multiple generations.

228
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:20,000
And the critic models
in this particular paper

229
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,040
was proposed as the critic
models will basically

230
00:11:24,040 --> 00:11:30,140
take the updated answer and
then select the best one.

231
00:11:30,140 --> 00:11:32,200
So they are also fine-tuned.

232
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:36,680
And these are fine-tuned on a
mix of trajectories where answer

233
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:38,560
is correct at the start
and then is corrected

234
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:40,220
over a course of debate.

235
00:11:40,220 --> 00:11:42,480
So basically, the
critic model is

236
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:47,360
learning how to contrast the
correct and the incorrect

237
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:49,480
answer.

238
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:52,120
So to summarize
the whole process,

239
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:55,120
there is n generation
models and they are each

240
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:56,720
producing an output.

241
00:11:56,720 --> 00:11:58,340
The summarization happens.

242
00:11:58,340 --> 00:12:01,340
You can imagine that either
you summarize it with a model,

243
00:12:01,340 --> 00:12:04,940
or you can just concatenate the
responses of all the models.

244
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:08,880
Then there is a
critique process on that

245
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:12,480
and that's added to the input.

246
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:15,160
And then you do a second
round for all of the model

247
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:18,600
generates outputs,
updated answers, and then

248
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:20,960
you do majority
voting on top of that

249
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:23,220
and again summarize
the responses.

250
00:12:23,220 --> 00:12:25,400
And you can continue
this process

251
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:27,480
in form of a debate
in some ways.

252
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:29,520
So those trajectories
can then be

253
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:35,160
used to fine-tune different
critiques as well.

254
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:37,400
So how does this help?

255
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:38,820
Why did we discuss this?

256
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:40,680
So I think the key
metric that we're

257
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:44,460
looking for is
that on the x-axis,

258
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:47,040
you're looking at how many
iterations of fine-tuning

259
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:49,648
it has gone through,
and on the y-axis,

260
00:12:49,648 --> 00:12:51,440
you either have the
negative log likelihood

261
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:54,880
or you have the
embeddings dissimilarity.

262
00:12:54,880 --> 00:12:58,560
The negative log likelihood is
just a proxy for performance.

263
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:04,800
And the embedding dissimilarity,
the higher the value,

264
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,000
the more diverse it is.

265
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,180
So what we see on the left is
for two open source models.

266
00:13:11,180 --> 00:13:13,078
When they go and do
fine-tuning, they're

267
00:13:13,078 --> 00:13:15,120
actually able to continue
to increase performance

268
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:17,500
when they do this
multi-agent fine-tuning.

269
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:22,008
And Llama seems more
responsive to this technique.

270
00:13:22,008 --> 00:13:23,800
I think the right side
is more interesting.

271
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:27,400
As they're going
through this process,

272
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:30,400
the responses continue
to stay quite diverse.

273
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:33,840
And this is over
math as a data set.

274
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:36,960
And overall, they
tried this again

275
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:39,440
in math over three
open source models.

276
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:41,590
And since they were
going with fine-tuning,

277
00:13:41,590 --> 00:13:43,340
they had to stick with
open-source models.

278
00:13:43,340 --> 00:13:45,400
But with multi-agent
techniques, they

279
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:48,360
were able to continue
to see improvements even

280
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:50,760
over multiple steps
of fine tuning.

281
00:13:50,760 --> 00:13:53,920
While for single-agent
fine-tuning,

282
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:58,760
the accuracy collapses or
doesn't continue to improve.

283
00:13:58,760 --> 00:14:03,880
And they also show that not
just in domain, which is math,

284
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:06,540
in adjacent domains
like GSM 8-k,

285
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:08,340
this is a slightly
older piece of work,

286
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:10,540
so the numbers
are not that high.

287
00:14:10,540 --> 00:14:14,740
But even in adjacent
domains like GSM 8-k,

288
00:14:14,740 --> 00:14:18,680
the fine-tuned agents actually
show higher performance.

289
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:22,040
So this technique
actually does help,

290
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:25,480
so it generalizes beyond
just in domain data

291
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:27,760
sets that it's trained on.

292
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:30,680
So that's the first
set of comments.

293
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:33,080
So what that roughly
says is that if you want

294
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:35,320
self-improvement, the
reasoning chains that

295
00:14:35,320 --> 00:14:37,920
are provided to the
model to drive those

296
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:39,500
need to be diverse in some way.

297
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:41,640
And how you generate
that one technique could

298
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:44,520
be based on having multiple
models or multiple agents

299
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:47,080
generate those kind of chains.

300
00:14:47,080 --> 00:14:50,100
A second problem that's
very challenging,

301
00:14:50,100 --> 00:14:53,440
and we covered actually
in the first few lectures

302
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:54,740
is around verification.

303
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:58,680
So it's quite hard to
verify model outputs.

304
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:03,340
And typically, you would
just look at the outcome.

305
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:08,940
So if you have a math
problem or a theorem proof,

306
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:10,940
what you will look at
is the final outcome.

307
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:12,700
And then you have an
outcome reward model,

308
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:14,260
so does that match
the ground truth,

309
00:15:14,260 --> 00:15:16,540
and then you will say
that this verifies.

310
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:20,680
One of the questions that we
discussed in the verification

311
00:15:20,680 --> 00:15:23,120
lecture is that what happens
if the reasoning chains are

312
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:26,140
wrong, and will that
lead the model astray,

313
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:31,400
or will that cause some
performance gap at the end?

314
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:34,440
And we also realize that
process reward models

315
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:35,660
are quite hard to build.

316
00:15:35,660 --> 00:15:38,240
So DeepSeekMath-V2
is a recent paper

317
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:40,080
that came out
which tries to show

318
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:42,300
that, at least in the
theorem proving domain,

319
00:15:42,300 --> 00:15:46,160
it is possible to build
self-verification loops, which

320
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:47,400
are automated.

321
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:51,960
So that's a very
interesting concept.

322
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,168
Just to remind you
on what I was saying,

323
00:15:54,168 --> 00:15:55,960
the current reinforcement
learning approach

324
00:15:55,960 --> 00:15:59,140
assumes that final answer
will match the ground truth,

325
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:02,380
and that's how you're
basically creating the rewards.

326
00:16:02,380 --> 00:16:05,040
So this has enabled saturation
of multiple benchmarks

327
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:08,460
like Amy or other
math-related benchmarks.

328
00:16:08,460 --> 00:16:11,512
But oftentimes, even when
you have the correct answer,

329
00:16:11,512 --> 00:16:13,220
you might not have
the correct reasoning.

330
00:16:13,220 --> 00:16:16,240
And if you are actually trying
to get the models to do better

331
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:18,040
in theorem proving,
you do require

332
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:19,940
rigorous step-by-step
derivation,

333
00:16:19,940 --> 00:16:23,480
which the final output
doesn't quite give you.

334
00:16:23,480 --> 00:16:26,880
Now, the interesting aspect is
that the large language models

335
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:29,580
are often trained on
quantitative reasoning,

336
00:16:29,580 --> 00:16:31,640
so the proofs that
they might generate

337
00:16:31,640 --> 00:16:33,300
are mathematically invalid.

338
00:16:33,300 --> 00:16:37,160
And if you ask them
to verify, they

339
00:16:37,160 --> 00:16:39,700
will claim that the
incorrect proofs are valid.

340
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:43,620
So LLM as a judge technique
doesn't quite work here.

341
00:16:43,620 --> 00:16:47,920
But if you actually have expert
humans look at these proofs,

342
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:52,218
they will be able to look at the
proof and reason about the fact

343
00:16:52,218 --> 00:16:53,260
that they know this area.

344
00:16:53,260 --> 00:16:55,940
They can reason about the
fact and say that, OK,

345
00:16:55,940 --> 00:16:56,940
this proof has issues.

346
00:16:56,940 --> 00:17:00,960
It's just not making sense
because this next step is not

347
00:17:00,960 --> 00:17:05,240
following from the last step
or there's reasoning gaps

348
00:17:05,240 --> 00:17:07,720
in the proof solution.

349
00:17:07,720 --> 00:17:11,040
So what DeepSeekMath-V2
proposes is

350
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:15,900
that instead of just
training the reward models,

351
00:17:15,900 --> 00:17:19,819
they train verifiers or
meta verifiers basically.

352
00:17:19,819 --> 00:17:23,599
So they get humans to
identify issues in the proofs

353
00:17:23,599 --> 00:17:25,260
without any reference solutions.

354
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:28,880
And based on that,
they train LLMs

355
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:34,080
to identify issues in the
proof and improve upon those.

356
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:37,440
And then if you will
find that there-- so

357
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:39,440
you basically have trained
a model which can now

358
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:41,890
critique the proofs themselves.

359
00:17:41,890 --> 00:17:46,017
So what the final architecture
for DeepSeekMath-V2 looks like,

360
00:17:46,017 --> 00:17:47,850
you folks are already
familiar with the fact

361
00:17:47,850 --> 00:17:49,870
that there's a generator
and a verifier,

362
00:17:49,870 --> 00:17:53,770
typically in the loop for
building train-time scaling

363
00:17:53,770 --> 00:17:54,270
system.

364
00:17:54,270 --> 00:17:57,970
So you have one model
that is producing proofs

365
00:17:57,970 --> 00:18:01,790
and another model that is
perhaps verifying things.

366
00:18:01,790 --> 00:18:03,250
So it's identifying issues.

367
00:18:03,250 --> 00:18:06,990
What DeepSeekMath-V2 adds is
this notion of meta verifier.

368
00:18:06,990 --> 00:18:10,090
So you have a meta
verifier that will

369
00:18:10,090 --> 00:18:13,357
review the verifiers analysis
for whether it makes sense

370
00:18:13,357 --> 00:18:15,190
or whether there are
issues with the proofs.

371
00:18:15,190 --> 00:18:19,010
And then the verifier is
trained to take these issues

372
00:18:19,010 --> 00:18:23,690
and then score the proofs
on a scale of 0.5 and 1.

373
00:18:23,690 --> 00:18:25,970
So the verifier
will then go improve

374
00:18:25,970 --> 00:18:27,530
the generator and
the generator will

375
00:18:27,530 --> 00:18:29,530
produce harder proofs,
which will then

376
00:18:29,530 --> 00:18:30,710
go improve the verifier.

377
00:18:30,710 --> 00:18:35,410
So basically this ends up
being a loop in some way.

378
00:18:35,410 --> 00:18:37,410
And the other
interesting aspect here

379
00:18:37,410 --> 00:18:40,930
is that because once
you see the data

380
00:18:40,930 --> 00:18:44,530
for finding incorrect
proofs, then you can actually

381
00:18:44,530 --> 00:18:46,270
automate that kind of labeling.

382
00:18:46,270 --> 00:18:48,850
So you don't have to just
rely on humans identifying

383
00:18:48,850 --> 00:18:52,330
issues in the proofs once
the meta verifier starts

384
00:18:52,330 --> 00:18:55,050
to learn this.

385
00:18:55,050 --> 00:18:57,810
So this notion of
meta-verification

386
00:18:57,810 --> 00:19:00,050
is generally quite
interesting in that

387
00:19:00,050 --> 00:19:04,050
verifiers can get correct score
when the reasoning chains are

388
00:19:04,050 --> 00:19:04,830
incorrect.

389
00:19:04,830 --> 00:19:09,030
For example, they might come
up with fabricated errors.

390
00:19:09,030 --> 00:19:13,170
So meta-verification has
evaluation of this analysis

391
00:19:13,170 --> 00:19:17,570
to these issues that the
verifier-- verifier here

392
00:19:17,570 --> 00:19:21,210
is LLM as a judge, or do these
identified issues actually

393
00:19:21,210 --> 00:19:21,790
exist?

394
00:19:21,790 --> 00:19:24,125
Does the score follow
from the issue?

395
00:19:24,125 --> 00:19:26,250
So they're basically
analyzing whether the verifier

396
00:19:26,250 --> 00:19:27,650
did a good job or not.

397
00:19:27,650 --> 00:19:29,530
And then they have
experts annotate

398
00:19:29,530 --> 00:19:32,130
the quality of this evaluation.

399
00:19:32,130 --> 00:19:34,728
And just by adding
this additional block,

400
00:19:34,728 --> 00:19:36,270
they are able to
improve the quality.

401
00:19:36,270 --> 00:19:39,330
So it's almost like you
had reasoning chains

402
00:19:39,330 --> 00:19:43,730
and then now you have the
verifier over those reasoning

403
00:19:43,730 --> 00:19:45,630
chains, which is
basically saying that,

404
00:19:45,630 --> 00:19:48,290
is this evaluation
correct or not?

405
00:19:48,290 --> 00:19:53,290
So there's an LLM as a judge,
which is identifying the issues

406
00:19:53,290 --> 00:19:54,470
in this reasoning chain.

407
00:19:54,470 --> 00:19:57,330
And then do those
issues exist or not,

408
00:19:57,330 --> 00:20:02,410
is what meta-verification
is looking for.

409
00:20:02,410 --> 00:20:06,770
If you look at the
results here, just

410
00:20:06,770 --> 00:20:10,050
with this iterative
optimization, I think all of us

411
00:20:10,050 --> 00:20:12,730
are familiar that a
lot of the RL loop

412
00:20:12,730 --> 00:20:15,650
often is built on top of TRPO.

413
00:20:15,650 --> 00:20:17,650
In this particular case,
they're building on top

414
00:20:17,650 --> 00:20:19,590
of DeepSeek-V3-based models.

415
00:20:19,590 --> 00:20:23,810
So just the verification
generation loop now

416
00:20:23,810 --> 00:20:29,890
benchmarked on IMO
problems and CNML problems,

417
00:20:29,890 --> 00:20:31,770
the Gemini version
of that was presented

418
00:20:31,770 --> 00:20:34,230
by one of our guest lecturers.

419
00:20:34,230 --> 00:20:35,650
But this is an
open-source version

420
00:20:35,650 --> 00:20:38,130
that was released recently.

421
00:20:38,130 --> 00:20:43,430
They are able to show that in
eight iterations just past one,

422
00:20:43,430 --> 00:20:45,570
the proof score
continues to climb just

423
00:20:45,570 --> 00:20:47,130
with that iterative loop.

424
00:20:47,130 --> 00:20:49,930
And then if you have
best at 32-- best at 32

425
00:20:49,930 --> 00:20:54,370
means you pick the best solution
out of 32 generated proofs,

426
00:20:54,370 --> 00:20:58,850
there you are almost getting
to 42% in proof score

427
00:20:58,850 --> 00:21:01,490
for IMO shortlist of 2024.

428
00:21:01,490 --> 00:21:05,330
So this is quite promising as a
hill-climbing technique, which

429
00:21:05,330 --> 00:21:09,233
roughly suggests that there is
some promise if you can identify

430
00:21:09,233 --> 00:21:10,650
the issues in the
reasoning chains

431
00:21:10,650 --> 00:21:12,970
and actually push the
model or nudge the model

432
00:21:12,970 --> 00:21:15,770
towards correcting them.

433
00:21:15,770 --> 00:21:21,410
So overall, the insights here
are that the best proofs will

434
00:21:21,410 --> 00:21:23,670
achieve higher
verification scores,

435
00:21:23,670 --> 00:21:27,770
and the generator can learn to
differentiate higher quality

436
00:21:27,770 --> 00:21:29,230
proofs from flawed proofs.

437
00:21:29,230 --> 00:21:31,290
And the self-verification
as a result

438
00:21:31,290 --> 00:21:36,190
can basically have this
better improvement loop.

439
00:21:36,190 --> 00:21:38,390
So verification
can be a bottleneck

440
00:21:38,390 --> 00:21:41,610
and this is one way to
break that bottleneck.

441
00:21:41,610 --> 00:21:44,170
Overall, I think
if you were to try

442
00:21:44,170 --> 00:21:47,290
to generalize this technique
in other areas, what you're

443
00:21:47,290 --> 00:21:51,470
basically looking for is if
you have LLM-based verifiers,

444
00:21:51,470 --> 00:21:54,810
you need to find a way to
get them to identify issues

445
00:21:54,810 --> 00:21:56,190
without reference solutions.

446
00:21:56,190 --> 00:21:58,330
That's what this
paper is relying on.

447
00:21:58,330 --> 00:22:00,930
Then you need some form of
an additional block, which

448
00:22:00,930 --> 00:22:04,770
is meta-verification
so that it can reduce

449
00:22:04,770 --> 00:22:06,650
the chance of
hallucinated issues

450
00:22:06,650 --> 00:22:08,850
that were identified
by the verifier.

451
00:22:08,850 --> 00:22:11,810
And then you add an
additional incentive

452
00:22:11,810 --> 00:22:15,850
for the generator
to maximize quality

453
00:22:15,850 --> 00:22:17,102
through deliberate reasoning.

454
00:22:17,102 --> 00:22:18,810
So you're basically
improving the quality

455
00:22:18,810 --> 00:22:20,730
of the model responses here.

456
00:22:20,730 --> 00:22:23,490
So overall, this notion
of self-verification

457
00:22:23,490 --> 00:22:26,810
is introduced by
our DeepSeekMath-V2.

458
00:22:26,810 --> 00:22:31,130
And it starts to break the
bottleneck in verification

459
00:22:31,130 --> 00:22:35,250
if you can make that more
automated end to end.

460
00:22:35,250 --> 00:22:39,890
Of course, this is
still limited by domains

461
00:22:39,890 --> 00:22:47,170
where verification is easier
rather than more difficult.

462
00:22:47,170 --> 00:22:52,330
So we covered through what
is the diversity in reasoning

463
00:22:52,330 --> 00:22:58,010
chains and we discussed a
bit about the verification

464
00:22:58,010 --> 00:23:01,850
bottleneck in the
models where oftentimes

465
00:23:01,850 --> 00:23:07,150
to get the models to get over
the bottleneck of verification,

466
00:23:07,150 --> 00:23:09,990
you would need some form
of verification techniques.

467
00:23:09,990 --> 00:23:14,010
And DeepSeekMath-V2 publishes
that in open source.

468
00:23:14,010 --> 00:23:15,970
A third problem that
you have to solve

469
00:23:15,970 --> 00:23:23,490
is this notion of what data or
what prompts should I train on.

470
00:23:23,490 --> 00:23:25,210
So there's this very
interesting paper

471
00:23:25,210 --> 00:23:30,570
that has not seen
much use yet, but is

472
00:23:30,570 --> 00:23:35,610
quite promising and interesting
is that you basically

473
00:23:35,610 --> 00:23:38,050
have the models propose
the set of tasks

474
00:23:38,050 --> 00:23:40,970
that they should go
train on that are just

475
00:23:40,970 --> 00:23:43,530
at the edge of what
they have learned.

476
00:23:43,530 --> 00:23:48,570
So to remind you, typically in
the current AI training stack,

477
00:23:48,570 --> 00:23:52,110
you are either using
human-curated reasoning traces,

478
00:23:52,110 --> 00:23:55,050
so that's for the
supervised learning stage,

479
00:23:55,050 --> 00:23:58,270
or for reinforcement learning
with verifiable rewards,

480
00:23:58,270 --> 00:24:00,650
you are expecting
some experts to curate

481
00:24:00,650 --> 00:24:04,010
the question-answer pairs,
but that basically requires

482
00:24:04,010 --> 00:24:08,430
that if you're constructing
such a model in math,

483
00:24:08,430 --> 00:24:09,910
then you need math experts.

484
00:24:09,910 --> 00:24:12,590
If it's an IMO problems,
then you need IMO experts,

485
00:24:12,590 --> 00:24:17,290
or if it is encoding, then you
need strong software coders.

486
00:24:17,290 --> 00:24:22,970
So as the models continue to
surpass human intelligence,

487
00:24:22,970 --> 00:24:26,610
the ability to find more and
more experts and more and more

488
00:24:26,610 --> 00:24:30,530
such tasks starts
to be limiting.

489
00:24:30,530 --> 00:24:35,390
So what this paper proposes,
and this idea is still very new,

490
00:24:35,390 --> 00:24:39,010
is that a single model can both
propose tasks and then solve

491
00:24:39,010 --> 00:24:39,510
them.

492
00:24:39,510 --> 00:24:42,290
So it almost goes to
the other extreme of,

493
00:24:42,290 --> 00:24:48,190
we should not really need
an external source of data.

494
00:24:48,190 --> 00:24:54,210
So basically, we should not
need human-generated prompts

495
00:24:54,210 --> 00:24:55,610
to climb on.

496
00:24:55,610 --> 00:24:57,410
The model itself will
propose the tasks

497
00:24:57,410 --> 00:24:59,030
and then it will go solve them.

498
00:24:59,030 --> 00:25:03,050
And so that's a very
interesting set of ideas.

499
00:25:03,050 --> 00:25:06,390
And it is feasible
in certain domains.

500
00:25:06,390 --> 00:25:08,790
So they focus more on
coding as a domain.

501
00:25:08,790 --> 00:25:13,890
And they effectively construct
three types of tasks--

502
00:25:13,890 --> 00:25:16,030
abduction, deduction,
and induction.

503
00:25:16,030 --> 00:25:19,550
And I will explain this diagram
in the subsequent slides.

504
00:25:19,550 --> 00:25:21,370
But effectively,
what this is saying

505
00:25:21,370 --> 00:25:24,050
is that in the
proposal stage, they

506
00:25:24,050 --> 00:25:28,090
will construct the tasks
based on this task types

507
00:25:28,090 --> 00:25:30,810
and then come up with
a reward to select

508
00:25:30,810 --> 00:25:32,470
which tasks make sense.

509
00:25:32,470 --> 00:25:36,250
And in the solution stage,
they will verify and then have

510
00:25:36,250 --> 00:25:37,270
an accuracy reward.

511
00:25:37,270 --> 00:25:40,890
And then that can help give
a joint update on the model

512
00:25:40,890 --> 00:25:45,450
itself, as opposed to
just one-sided updates.

513
00:25:45,450 --> 00:25:48,070
So what is the proposer?

514
00:25:48,070 --> 00:25:49,950
How is the proposer
proposing tasks?

515
00:25:49,950 --> 00:25:51,030
That's quite interesting.

516
00:25:51,030 --> 00:25:53,910
So it's effectively based
on coding paradigms.

517
00:25:53,910 --> 00:25:58,770
So it's effectively saying that
you have deduction tasks where

518
00:25:58,770 --> 00:26:02,770
you are generating
a program and input

519
00:26:02,770 --> 00:26:06,310
and the environment is
executing to get output.

520
00:26:06,310 --> 00:26:10,890
So this is your usual come up
with a program and an input

521
00:26:10,890 --> 00:26:11,390
pair.

522
00:26:11,390 --> 00:26:15,290
And then the environment
will execute to get outputs.

523
00:26:15,290 --> 00:26:18,050
Abduction tasks-- they're
similar to deduction.

524
00:26:18,050 --> 00:26:21,730
You're generating the program
and inputs and environment again

525
00:26:21,730 --> 00:26:24,350
computes outputs, but
induction tasks are different.

526
00:26:24,350 --> 00:26:27,330
It samples an existing
program and then generates

527
00:26:27,330 --> 00:26:31,690
new inputs for it plus a natural
language message describing

528
00:26:31,690 --> 00:26:34,370
the function, and
the environment then

529
00:26:34,370 --> 00:26:39,250
goes and executes and
decides whether this is going

530
00:26:39,250 --> 00:26:40,790
in the right direction or not.

531
00:26:43,520 --> 00:26:45,770
And another interesting bit
that I should mention here

532
00:26:45,770 --> 00:26:49,630
is that the proposer will
be conditioned on past here.

533
00:26:49,630 --> 00:26:52,250
So I'll cover that
in a later slide

534
00:26:52,250 --> 00:26:54,290
because otherwise it
will get confusing.

535
00:26:54,290 --> 00:26:56,090
But it will be
basically conditioned

536
00:26:56,090 --> 00:26:58,970
on past-generated examples
that are explicitly

537
00:26:58,970 --> 00:27:02,050
prompted so that
they're basically

538
00:27:02,050 --> 00:27:05,370
added to promote diversity.

539
00:27:05,370 --> 00:27:08,550
So how do you select tasks?

540
00:27:08,550 --> 00:27:12,370
The proposer will get a
reward based on optimizing

541
00:27:12,370 --> 00:27:13,970
for task difficulty.

542
00:27:13,970 --> 00:27:18,450
So if when the solver
can-- so each of the tasks

543
00:27:18,450 --> 00:27:20,890
that are generated are
passed to the solver.

544
00:27:20,890 --> 00:27:26,310
And if the success rate is zero,
then you get a zero reward.

545
00:27:26,310 --> 00:27:28,530
And if the success
rate is non-zero,

546
00:27:28,530 --> 00:27:31,470
then you basically get 1
minus average success rate.

547
00:27:31,470 --> 00:27:34,090
And the kind of task
that you want to select

548
00:27:34,090 --> 00:27:36,690
are the ones that
are not trivial

549
00:27:36,690 --> 00:27:38,430
and the ones that
are not impossible.

550
00:27:38,430 --> 00:27:40,890
So there is some ability
to continue to learn.

551
00:27:40,890 --> 00:27:44,310
So you end up selecting tasks
that are of moderate difficulty,

552
00:27:44,310 --> 00:27:47,670
where solver will sometimes
succeed and sometimes fail.

553
00:27:47,670 --> 00:27:53,150
And this will generally help
with getting the model to learn.

554
00:27:53,150 --> 00:27:56,430
So that's the goal
of the proposer,

555
00:27:56,430 --> 00:28:00,730
is to generate tasks for
optimal task difficulty

556
00:28:00,730 --> 00:28:02,670
at a current set
of model weights.

557
00:28:02,670 --> 00:28:04,670
And as the model
becomes more capable,

558
00:28:04,670 --> 00:28:09,530
then the proposer should learn
to propose harder problems.

559
00:28:09,530 --> 00:28:11,270
Of course, if you
generate tasks,

560
00:28:11,270 --> 00:28:14,630
then you need to somehow make
sure that these are valid tasks.

561
00:28:14,630 --> 00:28:18,270
So in the coding abstraction,
one can run program integrity.

562
00:28:18,270 --> 00:28:20,210
So you can actually
execute these tasks

563
00:28:20,210 --> 00:28:22,330
and see whether
there are any errors.

564
00:28:22,330 --> 00:28:23,670
You can do safety checks.

565
00:28:23,670 --> 00:28:27,130
You can also make sure that
if you run these inputs

566
00:28:27,130 --> 00:28:29,970
multiple times, you get
exactly same outputs because

567
00:28:29,970 --> 00:28:31,370
in code that's possible.

568
00:28:31,370 --> 00:28:35,290
So the proposed tasks before it
goes into the training pipeline

569
00:28:35,290 --> 00:28:36,350
is validated.

570
00:28:36,350 --> 00:28:42,210
So that's one way to make sure
that the proposal doesn't just

571
00:28:42,210 --> 00:28:45,490
hack and come up
with garbage tasks.

572
00:28:45,490 --> 00:28:48,550
And in terms of-- there's
a buffer that is kept.

573
00:28:48,550 --> 00:28:52,130
So for every seed
triplet, there's

574
00:28:52,130 --> 00:28:56,210
an identity function like
basically the input-output

575
00:28:56,210 --> 00:28:59,750
and the program for each
of those triplet tasks,

576
00:28:59,750 --> 00:29:02,130
you're adding them
to the task buffer.

577
00:29:02,130 --> 00:29:06,610
And the proposal can sample
references from that buffer.

578
00:29:06,610 --> 00:29:09,930
So there is some sort of a
buffer that is kept along

579
00:29:09,930 --> 00:29:16,310
with how many times the model is
succeeding or failing on this.

580
00:29:16,310 --> 00:29:19,330
So this is basically leading
to this idea of curriculum

581
00:29:19,330 --> 00:29:22,250
learning that is
evolving over time.

582
00:29:22,250 --> 00:29:25,510
And why is this
exciting or interesting?

583
00:29:25,510 --> 00:29:29,850
I mean, on coding benchmarks,
this notion starts to let you

584
00:29:29,850 --> 00:29:35,122
hill-climb despite having much
human curated data which--

585
00:29:35,122 --> 00:29:36,830
humans can generate
a lot of coding data,

586
00:29:36,830 --> 00:29:40,010
but that after a while,
synthetic data or some--

587
00:29:40,010 --> 00:29:43,190
this is almost a play on
synthetic data in some ways,

588
00:29:43,190 --> 00:29:48,850
but it's generating that in the
loop at the task level as well.

589
00:29:48,850 --> 00:29:51,010
So what they show is
that they're able to get

590
00:29:51,010 --> 00:29:52,750
state-of-the-art on
coding benchmarks,

591
00:29:52,750 --> 00:29:56,490
even though they didn't have any
human-curated data on the prompt

592
00:29:56,490 --> 00:29:56,990
side.

593
00:29:56,990 --> 00:29:59,490
And they are able
to outperform models

594
00:29:59,490 --> 00:30:02,490
